; bufio, DECLARED BY HAND, PROGRAM FIRST. Every claim below was read off
; src/bufio/scan.go and src/bufio/bufio.go in the Go 1.27 toolchain. What is
; declared is what examples/lines/lines.oro calls; the header names the rest.
;
; THE ALGEBRA.
;   B = [0, 255], and LF = 10, CR = 13.
;
;   THE LINES OF A STREAM. Every x ∈ B* factors UNIQUELY as
;       x = u₀ LF u₁ LF … LF u_k      with no LF in any uᵢ,
;   because B* is free and LF is one letter: k is the number of LFs. The line
;   split is
;       λ(x) = (c(u₀), …, c(u_{k−1}))            when u_k = ε,
;       λ(x) = (c(u₀), …, c(u_{k−1}), c(u_k))    otherwise,
;   where c drops one trailing CR. So |λ(x)| = k + [u_k ≠ ε], a final LF ends a
;   line and does not begin one, and a token never contains LF. λ is not
;   injective: it forgets CRs before LFs and whether the last line was ended.
;
;   A SCANNER is a cursor into λ(x), read once, forward. Scan advances it and
;   answers whether a token is there; Text is the token under it. So the loop
;       while Scan() { … Text() … }
;   is the fold of its body over λ(x), an unfold driven by the host: a stream
;   in Gibbons's sense (origami programming), consumed as it is produced.
;   Scan is false exactly when the tokens ran out or reading failed, and Err
;   then tells which: nil at the end of the input, the first error otherwise.
;
;   THE BOUND. A token is under MaxScanTokenSize bytes: a line of 65,536 or
;   more fills the buffer before its LF is seen, and Scan stops with
;   ErrTooLong. So a program's line is at most 65,535 bytes, a fact of this
;   file as long as it does not declare Scanner.Buffer, which moves the bound.
;   cmd/build's TestLinesFoldsTheLineSplit runs both sides of it: a token of
;   65,535 bytes is read and one of 65,536 ends the scan.
;
;   A WRITER is a monoid action made lazy. Writing w₁, …, wₙ and then Flush
;   puts w₁·…·wₙ on the underlying writer: the same bytes the unbuffered
;   writes would put, in fewer, larger writes. Before Flush a suffix of them
;   is still in the buffer, so a program that exits without Flush loses it.
;   A write error is STICKY: "no more data will be accepted and all subsequent
;   writes, and Flush, will return the error". So a program need not test
;   every write; Flush reports the first failure.
;
; PURITY. Nothing here is pure. Scan moves the cursor, Text and Err read what
; Scan wrote (the law at-map broke, gotarget-2026-09-30 §13), and a
; constructor's result has identity.
;
; WHAT IS NOT DECLARED, and what stops it:
;   - Scanner.Bytes: its slice aliases the scanner's buffer and "may be
;     overwritten by a subsequent call to Scan". An `(array …)` is immutable,
;     so this is a BORROW that ends at the next Scan, and the language has no
;     borrow that a later call ends. Text copies, and is declared.
;   - Scanner.Split and the SplitFunc type: a callback, tiers 1 and 2, not
;     built. ScanLines, ScanWords, ScanRunes and ScanBytes are split functions,
;     callable directly but useful only through Split.
;   - Scanner.Buffer: declarable, and it would make the bound above false.
;   - Reader and ReadWriter, 30 methods: work, not walls, except ReadSlice,
;     ReadLine and Peek, which return the same kind of borrow as Bytes.
;   - the rest of Writer (Write, WriteByte, WriteRune, Available, Buffered,
;     Size, Reset, ReadFrom, AvailableBuffer): work.
;   - the error variables (ErrTooLong, …): package variables, as io.EOF is.
(target go
  (module go/bufio
    (type Scanner (host "*bufio.Scanner"))
    (type Writer  (host "*bufio.Writer"))

    (const MaxScanTokenSize 65536 (host "bufio.MaxScanTokenSize" (import "bufio")))

    ; The split function is ScanLines until Split says otherwise, and Split is
    ; not declared, so every Scanner a program holds splits lines.
    (sig NewScanner ((r go/io.Reader)) Scanner (host expr "bufio.NewScanner(%s)" (import "bufio")))
    ; The default size, 4096 bytes.
    (sig NewWriter ((w go/io.Writer)) Writer (host expr "bufio.NewWriter(%s)" (import "bufio")))

    (module Scanner
      (use result)
      (sig Scan ((self Scanner)) bool (host expr "%s.Scan()" (import "bufio")))
      ; The token as a new string of its bytes. Any bytes: a line of a file is
      ; not text until it is decoded (ADR 0030).
      (sig Text ((self Scanner)) bytestring (host expr "%s.Text()" (import "bufio")))
      (sig Err ((self Scanner)) (result.result (tuple) error) (host expr "%s.Err()" (import "bufio"))))

    (module Writer
      (use result)
      ; n < len p exactly when err is not nil, on both: io.Writer's law. The
      ; errors are STICKY: the first is kept, every later call returns it, and
      ; Flush reports it. A write's error is subsumed by Flush's, so the two
      ; writes keep (option error), which may be ignored, and Flush has the
      ; relevant 1 + E: the obligation is where the host reports (errors.md §7).
      (sig Write ((self Writer) (p (array (int 0 255)))) (tuple (int 0 9223372036854775807) (option error))
        (host expr "%s.Write(%s)" (import "bufio")))
      (sig WriteString ((self Writer) (s bytestring)) (tuple (int 0 9223372036854775807) (option error))
        (host expr "%s.WriteString(%s)" (import "bufio")))
      (sig Flush ((self Writer)) (result.result (tuple) error) (host expr "%s.Flush()" (import "bufio")))))

  (implements go/bufio.Writer go/io.Writer go/io.StringWriter))
